<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tamil-brahmi">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamil-Brahmi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamil-Brahmi is a variant of the Brahmi script used to write the Tamil language from around the 3rd or 2nd century BCE. It is found in inscriptions in caves used by Jain monks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and scratched on pottery, including finds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> ports in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. It is the ancestor of the later Tamil and Vatteluttu scripts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi.jpg" alt="Tamil-Brahmi" width="320" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Symphonicallyu · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tamil_Brahmi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
